{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "bulk-load-and-bulk-copy",
  "domain": "data-movement",
  "value": ["corrects-a-wrong-assumption"],
  "correction": "Agents write BULK INSERT, OPENROWSET(BULK ...) and bcp against a local file path exactly as they would on SQL Server, then reach for the SQL Server minimal-logging playbook, TABLOCK, a BULK_LOGGED recovery model, large batches, to make a slow load fast, when in fact Azure SQL Database's server-side bulk load path only ever reads from Blob Storage, a local path is refused with Msg 12713 before the file is even checked for existence, and every load, minimally logged or not, is still capped by a fixed per-service-tier transaction log generation rate enforced by the log rate governor, which throttles ingestion no matter how the load is written.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer stages the file where the service can actually read it and loads from there, or drives the load from a client that streams the rows over the connection, and treats the throughput question as a service tier limit rather than as hint tuning.",
    "task": "Our nightly job loads a 40 million row CSV into a staging table. On the old SQL Server box it ran like this and finished in about eleven minutes:\n\n```sql\nALTER DATABASE staging SET RECOVERY BULK_LOGGED;\nBULK INSERT dbo.stg_orders\nFROM 'D:\\feeds\\orders_20260901.csv'\nWITH (FORMAT = 'CSV', FIRSTROW = 2, TABLOCK, BATCHSIZE = 500000);\n```\n\nWe have just moved the database to Azure SQL Database and the same job does not work at all. The file is produced by an upstream system onto a file share that the job server mounts. Management wants the load back inside the same overnight window it used to fit in. Give us the load path we should be using and how to get the throughput back.",
    "states": [
      "The server side bulk load path on this service reads only from Azure Blob Storage, so the file has to be staged there before the engine can read it.",
      "The path is rejected before the file is looked for, so this is not a permissions, share mount or missing file problem.",
      "The bulk logged recovery model is not available on this service, so that half of the old recipe cannot be reproduced.",
      "Log generation is capped per service tier by a governor, so ingestion rate is bounded by the tier no matter how the load statement is written."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "The statement will read from the local or mapped path once the service account permissions, the share mount or a credential are corrected.",
      "Setting the recovery model to bulk logged and keeping TABLOCK will restore minimal logging and the old throughput on this service.",
      "Throughput here is governed mainly by how the load statement is written, so a large enough batch size with the right hints will match the old server.",
      "Copying the file to the machine the database runs on, or to a path visible to the engine, is a workable option on this service."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["write", "provision"],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "connect-from-python",
    "connect-from-dotnet",
    "connect-from-typescript-and-node",
    "design-azure-sql-schema",
    "sqlpackage-import-export",
    "diagnose-resource-pressure",
    "restore-and-recover",
    "skill-feedback"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "setup": [
      "a database reachable with an administrative connection",
      "a small CSV file and a target table whose columns match it",
      "for the Blob paths, an Azure Blob Storage container and either a database scoped credential built from a shared access signature or a data source the loader can reach anonymously"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "BULK INSERT and OPENROWSET(BULK ...) against a path on the local filesystem both fail with Msg 12713, and the same message appears whether or not the named file exists",
      "no WITH option on BULK INSERT, including TABLOCK and BATCHSIZE, changes the Msg 12713 outcome for a local path",
      "an ad hoc https Blob URL with no matching CREDENTIAL object fails with Msg 15151 naming the URL itself as the credential it could not find",
      "CREATE EXTERNAL DATA SOURCE with TYPE = BLOB_STORAGE is accepted with no credential attached, and a BULK INSERT through it reaches the network layer rather than being rejected outright",
      "ADMINISTER DATABASE BULK OPERATIONS is a permission this engine recognises at database scope, which is the Azure SQL Database spelling of SQL Server's ADMINISTER BULK OPERATIONS",
      "sys.dm_user_db_resource_governance exposes primary_max_log_rate, so the log rate cap the skill tells the reader to check is readable from inside the database",
      "bcp is present in the engine container image, not on the host, and its version banner runs without a live connection"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT * FROM OPENROWSET(BULK '/tmp/probe-bulk-load.csv', SINGLE_CLOB) AS x;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 12713
        },
        "why": "The local filesystem path is the habit this skill exists to break, and Msg 12713 is the refusal that proves bulk load on this service reads Blob Storage and nothing else. Measured 2026-08-29 on an engine reporting EngineEdition 5. If this ever stops failing, the correction at the top of the skill is stale."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT * FROM OPENROWSET(BULK '/tmp/probe-never-created.csv', SINGLE_CLOB) AS x;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 12713
        },
        "why": "The same refusal for a path that was never created. This is the probe that separates a platform rule from a missing file: without it, the first probe is equally consistent with the engine opening the file and failing, which is exactly the wrong conclusion an agent draws from the message text alone."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT * FROM OPENROWSET(BULK 'https://example.blob.core.windows.net/probe-container/data.csv', SINGLE_CLOB) AS x;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 15151
        },
        "why": "An ad hoc Blob URL with no credential object of that exact name fails with Msg 15151, which names the whole URL as the credential it could not find and therefore reads like an access denial when it is not one. Measured 2026-08-29. Pinning the number is what lets the skill keep saying it means no such credential object exists."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_bulk_target (c1 nvarchar(100) NULL);\nBULK INSERT dbo.probe_bulk_target FROM '/tmp/probe-bulk-load.csv'\n  WITH (FIELDTERMINATOR = ',', ROWTERMINATOR = '0x0a', TABLOCK, BATCHSIZE = 1000);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 12713
        },
        "why": "TABLOCK and BATCHSIZE are the two options an agent reaches for the moment a load is called slow, and neither one moves the local-path refusal. Measured 2026-08-29: the identical Msg 12713 with both options present. This is the probe that keeps the corresponding do-not rule honest rather than merely asserted."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE EXTERNAL DATA SOURCE probe_blob\n  WITH (TYPE = BLOB_STORAGE, LOCATION = 'https://example.blob.core.windows.net/probe-container');",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The DATA_SOURCE form is the only legal bulk-load route on this service, so the skill's headline statement is worthless if this DDL is not accepted. Measured 2026-08-29: accepted with no error and with no CREDENTIAL attached, which is also what makes the anonymous-container case in the body real. A failure here means the shape in SKILL.md cannot be pasted, and the skill has to be rewritten rather than retuned."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME(DB_NAME(), 'DATABASE', 'ADMINISTER DATABASE BULK OPERATIONS') AS int) AS has_bulk_permission;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "Microsoft Learn's BULK INSERT permissions section, fetched 2026-09-03, says Azure SQL Database requires INSERT and ADMINISTER DATABASE BULK OPERATIONS where SQL Server requires ADMINISTER BULK OPERATIONS. HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME returns NULL for a permission name that is not valid at the named scope, so a NULL here would mean the skill is quoting the SQL Server spelling and the permission bullet is wrong. A 0 would mean the name is right but the probe connection is not administrative."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT primary_max_log_rate FROM sys.dm_user_db_resource_governance;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 208
        },
        "why": "This probe exists to pin a container versus cloud difference that cost this skill a wrong instruction. Learn documents sys.dm_user_db_resource_governance and its primary_max_log_rate column for Azure SQL Database, and an earlier revision told the reader to run exactly this to read their tier's log rate ceiling. Measured 2026-09-03 on the container: Msg 208, Invalid object name. The view is not there. sys.dm_db_resource_stats is absent too, and sys.dm_internal_resource_governor_resource_pools does exist but returns zero rows, so the log rate governor is not observable locally at all. The instruction is correct in the cloud and impossible on the container, and the body now says so. If this probe ever starts succeeding, the container gained the cloud DMV and the body needs revisiting."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "/opt/mssql-tools18/bin/bcp -v",
        "where": "container",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The skill routes a whole class of loads to bcp and states that bcp ships inside the engine image rather than on the host, so where this probe runs is half the claim. Measured 2026-08-29, exit 0, version 18.6.0002.1. Run on the host it fails with command not found, which is the finding, not a bug. It says nothing about bcp reaching a connected state, which was never achieved against this engine."
      }
    ]
  },
